# Part D, Task 1 — Ramulator on the L2 miss stream

Ramulator 2.0a (`CMU-SAFARI/ramulator2`, tag `v2.0a` — `main` is 2.1 and has
replaced the YAML CLI with a Python API, so `./ramulator2 -f ddr4.yaml` only
exists on the 2.0 line). DDR4_8Gb_x8, 1 channel, 2 ranks, DDR4_3200AA,
FR-FCFS, all-bank refresh, open-row policy, RoBaRaCoCh mapping.

> **Status: the trace is synthetic.** The real stream has to come from gem5's
> CommMonitor, and gem5 is Part E. The numbers below bracket the answer with
> three constructed streams so the pipeline, config and stats extraction are
> verified; every number is replaced once the CommMonitor trace exists. The
> recipe for producing it is at the end.

## Two things in the handout that do not run as written

**The trace format matches no Ramulator frontend.** There are three, and the
handout's `0x7f2a4c00 R` is none of them:

| Frontend | Format | |
|---|---|---|
| `SimpleO3` | `<bubble_count> <load_addr> [<store_addr>]` | what the handout's YAML selects |
| `ReadWriteTrace` | `<R\|W> <addr_vec>` | R/W, but a comma-separated DRAM coordinate vector, not a flat address |
| `LoadStoreTrace` | `<LD\|ST> <address>` | what an L2 miss stream actually is |

The handout uses the op letters of `ReadWriteTrace`, the flat address of
`LoadStoreTrace`, the reverse token order of both, and declares `SimpleO3`,
which wants a third format entirely. `SimpleO3` is also wrong on its own terms:
it models a core *and an LLC*, so feeding it an L2 miss stream would filter the
misses through a second cache. `to_ramulator.py` converts the handout format to
`LD`/`ST` and the config uses `LoadStoreTrace`.

**Ramulator 2.0 has no write-latency statistic.** The controller registers
`read_latency_N` and `avg_read_latency_N` only
(`generic_dram_controller.cpp:109`). Writes are retired when the command
issues — there is no round trip to time — so the task's "total read/write
latency" can only be answered for reads. The write side is visible as
`num_write_reqs_0` and through its effect on read latency via bus turnaround.

The handout's YAML also omits `Translation`, `RowPolicy` and `AddrMapper`,
which have no defaults in 2.0 and must be supplied.

## Results

100 000 accesses each, 25 % writes (dirty writebacks from an 8-way writeback L2).

| Stream | memory_system_cycles | reads | writes | read_latency_0 | avg read latency | row-buffer hit rate |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| `seq` — sequential 64 B stride | 697 849 | 75 069 | 24 931 | 24 992 147 | 332.9 | **99.1 %** |
| `rand` — uniform over 256 MB | 519 900 | 75 069 | 24 931 | 21 767 213 | 290.0 | **0.1 %** |
| `mixed` — 4 streams + 30 % random | 779 326 | 75 036 | 24 964 | 28 540 021 | 380.4 | **58.9 %** |

Latencies are in DRAM command cycles; tCK = 625 ps at DDR4-3200, so 332.9 cycles
is 208 ns.

**The average is computed by hand, not read from `avg_read_latency_0`.** That
statistic is wrong in Ramulator 2.0a: `finalize()` divides `s_read_latency` by
`s_num_read_reqs`, but `s_num_read_reqs++` sits in `send()`
(`generic_dram_controller.cpp:118`) and fires on every send *attempt*, including
the ones rejected because the buffer is full. In the `seq` run that denominator
is 1 835 999 against 75 069 real reads, a 24× inflation, and the printed
`avg_read_latency_0` is 13.61 instead of 332.9. Use
`read_latency_0 / total_num_read_requests`. Task 2 shows this inverts the
FR-FCFS/FCFS comparison if taken at face value.

Row activity behind the hit rate:

| Stream | row hits | row misses | row conflicts |
|---|---|---|---|
| `seq` | 99 088 | 872 | 0 |
| `rand` | 57 | 8 424 | 91 479 |
| `mixed` | 58 828 | 4 547 | 36 571 |

## The result that is not obvious

**The stream with the best row-buffer hit rate is not the fastest.** `seq` hits
the row buffer 99.1 % of the time and takes 697 849 cycles; `rand` hits it 0.1 %
of the time and finishes in 519 900 — **25 % faster**.

The cause is the address mapping. `RoBaRaCoCh` puts column bits below bank and
rank bits, so consecutive 64 B lines land in consecutive columns of the *same
row of the same bank*. Sequential access therefore gets perfect row locality and
**zero bank-level parallelism** — every access serialises behind the previous
one on a single bank, and the 32 banks (2 ranks × 4 bank groups × 4 banks) sit
idle. Random access thrashes rows but spreads across all 32 banks, so activations
and precharges on one bank overlap with data transfer on another. Bank
parallelism is worth more here than row locality.

`mixed` is the worst of the three, 779 326 cycles, and that is consistent rather
than contradictory: its four sequential streams collide in the same banks and
repeatedly close each other's open rows, producing 36 571 row conflicts. A
conflict costs tRP + tRCD on a bank that was *already busy* — strictly worse than
a miss on an idle bank, which is what `rand` mostly generates.

Converted to delivered bandwidth against the channel's 25.6 GB/s peak
(3200 MT/s × 8 B):

| Stream | Time | Bandwidth | Channel utilisation |
|---|---|---|---|
| `seq` | 436.2 µs | 14.67 GB/s | 57.3 % |
| `rand` | 324.9 µs | 19.70 GB/s | 76.9 % |
| `mixed` | 487.1 µs | 13.14 GB/s | 51.3 % |

So a saturating L2 miss stream occupies **51–77 % of one DDR4-3200 channel**
depending only on its address pattern — which is the shape of the answer Part
D's central question needs, and the reason the real trace matters. The spread
between these bounds is larger than most of the effects Parts B and C measured.

## Generating the real trace

gem5 could not be built here — this container has 1 core and 3 GB of RAM. On
your machine:

```bash
git clone --depth 1 https://github.com/gem5/gem5.git
cd gem5 && pip install scons  # plus python3-devel, protobuf-devel, libpng-devel
scons build/X86/gem5.opt -j$(nproc)
```

Insert a `CommMonitor` between the L2 and the memory bus, and attach a trace
listener to it:

```python
from m5.objects import CommMonitor
mon = CommMonitor()
mon.trace = MemTraceProbe(trace_file="l2miss.trc.gz")
system.l2cache.mem_side = mon.cpu_side_port
mon.mem_side_port = system.membus.cpu_side_ports
```

gem5 writes a protobuf trace; decode it with
`util/decode_packet_trace.py l2miss.trc.gz l2miss.csv`, then keep the address
and command columns, emit `<addr> <R|W>` lines, and run them through
`to_ramulator.py`. Everything downstream of that is already in place — same
`ddr4.yaml`, same `run.sh`, same `stats.py`.
